{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba blueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex | sed -n '535,625p'\"","aggregated_output":"   535\t\t\\leanok\n   536\t\t\\uses{def:consistency, def:bracket, def:povm-conventions}\n   537\t\tLet $\\mathcal D$ be a distribution on $(x, y_1, y_2) \\in \\mathcal X \\times \\mathcal Y_1 \\times \\mathcal Y_2$. For $i \\in \\{1,2\\}$ let $\\mathcal G_i$ be a collection of functions $g_i \\colon \\mathcal Y_i \\to \\mathcal R_i$ and let $\\{(G_i)^x_g\\}_{g \\in \\mathcal G_i}$ be families of measurements such that $\\{(G_2)^x_g\\}_g$ is projective for every $x$. Suppose further that for every pair $(x, y_1)$ of positive probability under the marginal of $\\mathcal D$ on $\\mathcal X \\times \\mathcal Y_1$ (the only pairs for which the conditional distribution of $y_2$ under $\\mathcal D$ is defined; pairs of marginal probability zero are subject to no requirement) it holds that for $g_2 \\ne g_2' \\in \\mathcal G_2$ the probability, on average over $y_2$ chosen from $\\mathcal D$ conditioned on $(x, y_1)$, that $g_2(y_2) = g_2'(y_2)$ is at most $\\eta$. Let $\\{A^{x,y_1,y_2}_{a_1,\\,a_2}\\}$ be a family of projective measurements with outcomes $(a_1, a_2) \\in \\mathcal R_1 \\times \\mathcal R_2$ such that for $i \\in \\{1,2\\}$,\n   538\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:pasting-1}\n   539\t\t\tA^{x,y_1,y_2}_{a_i} \\ot \\Id \\simeq_\\delta \\Id \\ot (G_i)^x_{[\\operatorname{eval}_{y_i}(\\cdot)=a_i]}\n   540\t\t\\end{equation}\n   541\t\tand\n   542\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:pasting-2}\n   543\t\t\tA^{x,y_1,y_2}_{a_1,\\,a_2} \\ot \\Id \\simeq_\\delta \\Id \\ot A^{x,y_1,y_2}_{a_1,\\,a_2}\\,,\n   544\t\t\\end{equation}\n   545\t\twhere $A^{x,y_1,y_2}_{a_i}$ denotes the marginal on the $i$-th part of the outcome (Definition~\\ref{def:povm-conventions}). Define a family of measurements $\\{J^x_{g_1,\\,g_2}\\}$ by\n   546\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:pasting-2a}\n   547\t\t\tJ^x_{g_1,\\,g_2} = (G_2)^x_{g_2}\\, (G_1)^x_{g_1}\\, (G_2)^x_{g_2}\\,.\n   548\t\t\\end{equation}\n   549\t\tThen there is a\n   550\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:def-deltap}\n   551\t\t\t\\delta_{\\mathrm{pasting}} = \\delta_{\\mathrm{pasting}}(\\eta, \\delta) = \\poly(\\eta, \\delta)\n   552\t\t\\end{equation}\n   553\t\tsuch that\n   554\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:pasting-3}\n   555\t\t\tA^{x,y_1,y_2}_{a_1,\\,a_2} \\ot \\Id \\simeq_{\\delta_{\\mathrm{pasting}}} \\Id \\ot J^x_{[\\operatorname{eval}_{y_1}(\\cdot)=a_1,\\, \\operatorname{eval}_{y_2}(\\cdot)=a_2]}\\,.\n   556\t\t\\end{equation}\n   557\t\tNeither the exponent nor the constant in $\\poly(\\eta, \\delta)$ is made explicit in the source; both are universal constants inherited from the proof of Fact 4.35 in \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}.\n   558\t\\end{lemma}\n   559\t\\begin{proof}\n   560\t\tStated in the source without proof, as an import of Fact 4.35 of \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. The measurement $J^x$ sandwiches the family $(G_1)^x$ between two copies of the projective family $(G_2)^x$. The consistency hypotheses \\eqref{eq:pasting-1} and the self-consistency \\eqref{eq:pasting-2} allow the joint measurement $A^{x,y_1,y_2}$ to be compared with the sandwiched product one factor at a time, and the $\\eta$-bound on the pairwise agreement of distinct functions in $\\mathcal G_2$ controls the error terms arising when two different outcomes $g_2 \\ne g_2'$ agree at the sampled point $y_2$.\n   561\t\\end{proof}\n   562\t\n   563\t\\begin{lemma}[Formalization support for Lemma~\\ref{lem:pasting}]\n   564\t\t\\label{lem:pasting-measurement}\n   565\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.pastedMeasurement_isMeasurement}\n   566\t\t\\leanok\n   567\t\t\\uses{def:povm-conventions}\n   568\t\tThis formalization-only auxiliary is not a named statement of the paper; it\n   569\t\tisolates the assertion, left implicit in~\\eqref{eq:pasting-2a}, that the\n   570\t\tpasted family is a POVM. If $\\{(G_1)^x_{g_1}\\}_{g_1}$ is a measurement and\n   571\t\t$\\{(G_2)^x_{g_2}\\}_{g_2}$ is a projective measurement, then every operator\n   572\t\t$J^x_{g_1,\\,g_2}$ is positive and\n   573\t\t$\\sum_{g_1,\\,g_2} J^x_{g_1,\\,g_2} = \\Id$.\n   574\t\\end{lemma}\n   575\t\\begin{proof}\n   576\t\t\\leanok \\uses{lem:distance-measurement-effect-hermitian}\n   577\t\tPositivity holds because $J^x_{g_1,\\,g_2}$ is the conjugation of the\n   578\t\tpositive operator $(G_1)^x_{g_1}$ by the self-adjoint operator\n   579\t\t$(G_2)^x_{g_2}$. Summing over $g_1$ first leaves\n   580\t\t$\\sum_{g_2} (G_2)^x_{g_2} (G_2)^x_{g_2}$, which equals\n   581\t\t$\\sum_{g_2} (G_2)^x_{g_2} = \\Id$ by idempotence and completeness.\n   582\t\\end{proof}\n   583\t\n   584\t\\begin{lemma}[Close strategies have close values; Fact 4.32 in \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}]\\label{lem:close-strategies-have-close-values}\n   585\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.abs_value_sub_le_of_areClose}\n   586\t\t\\leanok\n   587\t\t\\uses{def:strategy-distance, def:tensor-product-value, def:projective-strategy-general}\n   588\t\tLet $\\mathfrak G$ be a nonlocal game, and let $\\mathcal S$, $\\mathcal S'$ be two strategies that are $\\delta$-close in the sense of Definition~\\ref{def:strategy-distance}, for some $\\delta \\in [0,1]$, and that use the same state $\\ket{\\psi}$. If either $\\mathcal S$ or $\\mathcal S'$ is projective, then\n   589\t\t\\[\n   590\t\t\t\\bigl| \\operatorname{val}^*(\\mathfrak G, \\mathcal S) - \\operatorname{val}^*(\\mathfrak G, \\mathcal S') \\bigr| \\le O(\\delta^{1/2})\\,.\n   591\t\t\\]\n   592\t\\end{lemma}\n   593\t\\begin{proof}\n   594\t\t\\leanok\n   595\t\t\\uses{fact:triangle}\n   596\t\tStated in the source without proof; it is Fact 4.32 of \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. Write the value difference as an average over $(x,y) \\sim \\mu$ of sums of terms of the form $\\bra{\\psi} (A^x_a - (A')^x_a) \\ot B^y_b \\ket{\\psi}$ and $\\bra{\\psi} (A')^x_a \\ot (B^y_b - (B')^y_b) \\ket{\\psi}$, replacing one player's measurements at a time. The Cauchy--Schwarz inequality bounds each group by the square root of the corresponding averaged squared state-dependent distance, and projectivity of one of the two strategies keeps the accompanying operator sums bounded, giving the total bound $O(\\delta^{1/2})$.\n   597\t\\end{proof}\n   598\t\n   599\t\\paragraph{Formalization support for the distance calculus.}\n   600\tThe following subordinate results isolate the finite-dimensional estimates and\n   601\ttensor identities used in the preceding proofs. They are not named statements\n   602\tof the paper.\n   603\t\n   604\t\\begin{lemma}[Formalization support for Fact~\\ref{fact:add-a-proj}]\n   605\t\t\\label{lem:distance-quadratic-form-mono}\n   606\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.DistanceCalculus.quadratic_form_mono}\n   607\t\t\\leanok\n   608\t\tThis formalization-only auxiliary is not a named statement of the paper; it\n   609\t\tisolates the order step in Fact~\\ref{fact:add-a-proj}. If $S \\leq T$, then\n   610\t\t$\\operatorname{Re}\\langle v,Sv\\rangle \\leq\n   611\t\t\\operatorname{Re}\\langle v,Tv\\rangle$ for every vector $v$.\n   612\t\\end{lemma}\n   613\t\\begin{proof}\n   614\t\t\\leanok\n   615\t\tApply positivity of $T-S$ to $v$ and expand $T=S+(T-S)$.\n   616\t\\end{proof}\n   617\t\n   618\t\\begin{lemma}[Formalization support for Fact~\\ref{fact:add-a-proj}]\n   619\t\t\\label{lem:distance-sum-contraction}\n   620\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.DistanceCalculus.sum_norm_mul_apply_le}\n   621\t\t\\leanok\n   622\t\tThis formalization-only auxiliary is not a named statement of the paper; it\n   623\t\tisolates the contraction estimate in Fact~\\ref{fact:add-a-proj}. If\n   624\t\t$\\sum_c C_c^\\dagger C_c\\leq\\Id$, then\n   625\t\t\\[\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
