{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_32","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba blueprint/src/chapter/ch14_qpbt_observables.tex | sed -n '385,480p'\"","aggregated_output":"   385\t\n   386\tThe following lemma shows that POVMs that are approximately projective, in\n   387\tthe sense of being self-consistent, are close to exact projective\n   388\tmeasurements.\n   389\t\n   390\t\\begin{lemma}[Orthonormalization lemma]\n   391\t\t\\label{lem:ortho}\n   392\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.exists_projective_close_of_consistent}\n   393\t\t\\leanok\n   394\t\t\\uses{def:consistency, def:povm-distance}\n   395\t\tLet $0 \\leq \\delta \\leq 1$. There exists a function\n   396\t\t$\\eta_{\\mathrm{ortho}}(\\delta) = O\\big(\\delta^{1/4}\\big)$ such that the\n   397\t\tfollowing holds. Let $\\ket{\\psi}$ be a state on\n   398\t\t$\\hilb_{\\mathsf{A}} \\ot \\hilb_{\\mathsf{B}}$ where $\\hilb_{\\mathsf{A}}$\n   399\t\tand $\\hilb_{\\mathsf{B}}$ are finite dimensional, and let $\\{Q_a\\}$ and\n   400\t\t$\\{R_a\\}$ be POVMs on $\\hilb_{\\mathsf{A}}$ and $\\hilb_{\\mathsf{B}}$\n   401\t\trespectively such that\n   402\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:cons-cond}\n   403\t\t\tQ_a \\ot \\Id_{\\mathsf{B}} \\,\\simeq_\\delta\\, \\Id_{\\mathsf{A}} \\ot R_a\\,.\n   404\t\t\\end{equation}\n   405\t\tThen there exists a projective measurement $\\{P_a\\}$ on\n   406\t\t$\\hilb_{\\mathsf{A}}$ such that, with respect to $\\ket{\\psi}$,\n   407\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:cons-ccl}\n   408\t\t\tP_a \\ot \\Id_{\\mathsf{B}} \\,\\approx_{\\eta_{\\mathrm{ortho}}(\\delta)}\\, Q_a \\ot \\Id_{\\mathsf{B}}\\,.\n   409\t\t\\end{equation}\n   410\t\\end{lemma}\n   411\t\\begin{proof}\n   412\t\t\\leanok\n   413\t\t\\uses{lem:ortho-explicit-constant}\n   414\t\tThe source imports this lemma: it first appears in~\\cite{KV11}, and a\n   415\t\tself-contained proof is given in the reference [ML20] cited\n   416\t\tin~\\cite{Ji2020MIPStar}. Here it is obtained from\n   417\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:ortho-explicit-constant}, which exhibits\n   418\t\t$\\eta_{\\mathrm{ortho}}(\\delta) = 220\\,\\delta^{1/4}$: passing to Alice's\n   419\t\treduced state, the rank-reduction and orthonormalization construction of\n   420\t\tChapter~\\ref{chap:projective} produces a projective family on\n   421\t\t$\\hilb_{\\mathsf{A}}$ close to $\\{Q_a\\}$, and completing that family at one\n   422\t\tfixed outcome yields the projective measurement $\\{P_a\\}$.\n   423\t\\end{proof}\n   424\t\n   425\t\\begin{lemma}[Formalization support for Lemma~\\ref{lem:ortho}]\n   426\t\t\\label{lem:ortho-explicit-constant}\n   427\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.projective_rounding_with_explicit_constant}\n   428\t\t\\leanok\n   429\t\t\\uses{def:consistency, def:povm-distance}\n   430\t\tThis formalization-only auxiliary is not a named statement of the paper; it is\n   431\t\tthe explicit-constant form of Lemma~\\ref{lem:ortho} from which that lemma is\n   432\t\tobtained. Let $\\ket{\\psi}$ be a unit vector on\n   433\t\t$\\hilb_{\\mathsf{A}} \\ot \\hilb_{\\mathsf{B}}$ with $\\hilb_{\\mathsf{A}}$ and\n   434\t\t$\\hilb_{\\mathsf{B}}$ finite dimensional, let $\\{Q_a\\}$ and $\\{R_a\\}$ be POVMs\n   435\t\ton $\\hilb_{\\mathsf{A}}$ and $\\hilb_{\\mathsf{B}}$ respectively, and let\n   436\t\t$\\delta \\geq 0$ satisfy\n   437\t\t\\[\n   438\t\t\tQ_a \\ot \\Id_{\\mathsf{B}} \\,\\simeq_\\delta\\, \\Id_{\\mathsf{A}} \\ot R_a\\,.\n   439\t\t\\]\n   440\t\tThen there is a projective measurement $\\{P_a\\}$ on $\\hilb_{\\mathsf{A}}$ with\n   441\t\t\\[\n   442\t\t\td_\\psi\\bigl(P_a \\ot \\Id_{\\mathsf{B}},\\, Q_a \\ot \\Id_{\\mathsf{B}}\\bigr)^2\n   443\t\t\t\\leq 220\\,\\delta^{1/4}\\,.\n   444\t\t\\]\n   445\t\\end{lemma}\n   446\t\\begin{proof}\n   447\t\t\\leanok\n   448\t\t\\uses{lem:projective-low-rank-sum, lem:Q-completeness, lem:P-Q-approx,\n   449\t\t\tdef:projective-P}\n   450\t\tWork with Alice's reduced state $\\varphi$, for which the consistency\n   451\t\thypothesis gives $\\sum_a \\bra{\\varphi} Q_a - Q_a^2 \\ket{\\varphi} \\leq 2\\delta$.\n   452\t\tWhen $\\delta^{1/4} \\leq 1/24$, rank reduction\n   453\t\t(Lemma~\\ref{lem:projective-low-rank-sum}) replaces $\\{Q_a\\}$ by a family of\n   454\t\tprojectors, Lemma~\\ref{lem:Q-completeness} shows that this family is almost\n   455\t\tcomplete on $\\varphi$, and the operator $P$ of\n   456\t\tDefinition~\\ref{def:projective-P} is projective and close to it by\n   457\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:P-Q-approx}. Completing $P$ at one fixed outcome and summing the\n   458\t\ttwo estimates gives the bound $220\\,\\delta^{1/4}$, the completion contributing\n   459\t\tthe residual mass controlled by Lemma~\\ref{lem:Q-completeness}. When\n   460\t\t$\\delta^{1/4} > 1/24$, the bound $220\\,\\delta^{1/4} > 9$ exceeds the universal\n   461\t\tbound $4$ on the distance between any two sub-measurements on a normalized\n   462\t\tstate, so the completion of the zero sub-measurement already satisfies the\n   463\t\tconclusion.\n   464\t\\end{proof}\n   465\t\n   466\t\\section{Strategies}\n   467\t\\label{sec:commutation}\n   468\t\n   469\t% In the source this setup --- fixing the parameter tuple, the strategy and\n   470\t% its success probability, and making the measurements projective via\n   471\t% Naimark's theorem --- is untitled prose at the head of the subsection\n   472\t% (references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex, lines\n   473\t% 155-171), not a numbered environment. It is recorded here as a standing\n   474\t% convention paragraph together with a lemma for the one mathematical step\n   475\t% it contains, the reduction to a projective strategy.\n   476\t\\paragraph{The strategy under analysis.}\n   477\tFor the remainder of this chapter, as well as in\n   478\tChapters~\\ref{chap:qpbt-combining} and~\\ref{chap:qpbt-extraction}, we fix\n   479\tan admissible parameter tuple $(q,m,d)$\n   480\t(Definition~\\ref{def:admissible}) and a strategy\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
