{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_129","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n 'tensorMeasurement|Measurement.*tensor|kronecker.*Measurement|Measurement.*kronecker|prod.*Measurement' MIPStarRE .lake/packages/mathlib/Mathlib/QuantumInfo .lake/packages/mathlib/Mathlib/LinearAlgebra 2>/dev/null\"","aggregated_output":"MIPStarRE/LDT/Basic/MeasurementLift.lean:6:Measurement-level tensor-factor lifts built from the submeasurement placement API.\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Games/DistanceTheorems.lean:281:private noncomputable def tensorMeasurement\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Games/DistanceTheorems.lean:308:private theorem tensorMeasurement_isProjective\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Games/DistanceTheorems.lean:314:    MIPStarRE.QPBT.Measurement.IsProjective (tensorMeasurement A B) := by\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Games/DistanceTheorems.lean:426:      (fun xy ab => (tensorMeasurement (A xy.1) (B xy.2)).effect ab)\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Games/DistanceTheorems.lean:427:      (fun xy ab => (tensorMeasurement (A' xy.1) (B xy.2)).effect ab) ψ ≤ δ := by\nMIPStarRE/QPBT/Games/DistanceTheorems.lean[Distance between strategies]\\label{def:strategy-distance}\n   244\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.AreCloseStrategies}\n   245\t\t\\leanok\n   246\t\t\\uses{def:tensor-product-strategy, def:state-distance, def:povm-distance}\n   247\t\tLet $\\mathfrak G = (\\mathcal X, \\mathcal Y, \\mathcal A, \\mathcal B, \\mu, D)$ be a nonlocal game and let $\\mathcal S = (\\psi, A, B)$ and $\\mathcal S' = (\\psi', A', B')$ be strategies for $\\mathfrak G$. For $\\delta \\in [0,1]$ we say that \\emph{$\\mathcal S$ is $\\delta$-close to $\\mathcal S'$} if the following conditions hold.\n   248\t\t\\begin{enumerate}\n   249\t\t\t\\item The states $\\ket{\\psi}, \\ket{\\psi'}$ are states in the same Hilbert space $\\mathcal H_A \\ot \\mathcal H_B$ and are $\\delta$-close.\n   250\t\t\t\\item For all $x \\in \\mathcal X$ and $y \\in \\mathcal Y$, we have $A^x_a \\approx_\\delta (A')^x_a$ and $B^y_b \\approx_\\delta (B')^y_b$, with the approximations holding under the distribution $\\mu$ (i.e.\\ under its corresponding marginals), on either $\\ket{\\psi}$ or $\\ket{\\psi'}$.\n   251\t\t\\end{enumerate}\n   252\t\\end{definition}\n   253\t\n   254\t\\begin{remark}[Asymptotic reading of the distance relations]\\label{rem:asymptotic-distance}\n   255\t\tDefinitions~\\ref{def:state-distance}, \\ref{def:consistency}, and \\ref{def:povm-distance} are asymptotic: every $\\approx_\\delta$ and $\\simeq_\\delta$ statement refers to families of states and measurement families indexed by an implicit parameter $n \\in \\N$, and the $O(\\cdot)$ is taken as $n \\to \\infty$. None of these relations is a pointwise inequality with an explicit constant. By contrast, the corresponding relations used in the low individual degree chapters (Definitions~\\ref{def:simeq} and~\\ref{def:approx_delta}) carry no hidden constant. Any explicit quantitative version of the present relations therefore retains the constants absorbed into the $O(\\cdot)$; for instance, the triangle inequality of Lemma~\\ref{fact:triangle} carries a factor $2$ inside the $O(\\cdot)$.\n   256\t\\end{remark}\n   257\t\n   258\tWe record several useful facts about the consistency measure and the state-dependent distance. For the statements imported without proof, readers are referred to Sections~4.4 and~4.5 of \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}.\n   259\t\n   260\t\\begin{lemma}[Consistency versus closeness; Fact 4.13 in \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}]\\label{fact:agreement}\n   261\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.opFamilyDistSq_le_two_mul_consistencyDefect, MIPStarRE.QPBT.consistencyDefect_le_opFamilyDistSq_of_projective}\n   262\t\t\\leanok\n   263\t\t\\uses{def:consistency, def:povm-distance}\n   264\t\tFor POVMs $\\{A^x_a\\}$ and $\\{B^x_a\\}$, the following hold.\n   265\t\t\\begin{enumerate}\n   266\t\t\t\\item If $A^x_a \\ot \\Id_B \\simeq_\\delta \\Id_A \\ot B^x_a$, then $A^x_a \\ot \\Id_B \\approx_\\delta \\Id_A \\ot B^x_a$.\n   267\t\t\t\\item If $A^x_a \\ot \\Id_B \\approx_\\delta \\Id_A \\ot B^x_a$ \\emph{and} $\\{A^x_a\\}$ and $\\{B^x_a\\}$ are projective measurements, then $A^x_a \\ot \\Id_B \\simeq_\\delta \\Id_A \\ot B^x_a$.\n   268\t\t\\end{enumerate}\n   269\t\\end{lemma}\n   270\t\\begin{proof}\n   271\t\t\\leanok\n   272\t\t\\uses{lem:distance-consistency-term,\n   273\t\t\tlem:distance-point-distance-le-defect,\n   274\t\t\tlem:distance-projective-point-distance, lem:distance-nonnegative}\n   275\t\tStated in the source without proof; this is Fact 4.13 of \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. For the first item, expand each term $\\lVert (A^x_a \\ot \\Id - \\Id \\ot B^x_a)\\ket{\\psi} \\rVert^2$ and use $ (A^x_a)^2 \\le A^x_a$, $(B^x_a)^2 \\le B^x_a$ together with completeness of the two POVMs: summing over $a$ and averaging over $x$ bounds the total by at most twice the off-diagonal mass, which is $O(\\delta)$ by hypothesis. When both families are projective the two operator inequalities hold with equality, so the expansion equals exactly twice the off-diagonal mass; reading this identity in the other direction gives the second item.\n   276\t\\end{proof}\n   277\t\n   278\t\\begin{lemma}[Consistency from closeness with one projective family; Fact 4.14 in \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}]\\label{fact:agreement-one-sided}\n   279\t\t% Status: the statement is final; no Lean declaration exists yet for the full disjunctive form, so this node carries no formalization marker.\n   280\t\t\\uses{def:consistency, def:povm-distance}\n   281\t\tLet $\\{A^x_a\\}$ and $\\{B^x_a\\}$ be POVMs. If $A^x_a \\ot \\Id_B \\approx_\\delta \\Id_A \\ot B^x_a$ and either $\\{A^x_a\\}$ or $\\{B^x_a\\}$ is a projective measurement, then $A^x_a \\ot \\Id_B \\simeq_{\\delta^{1/2}} \\Id_A \\ot B^x_a$.\n   282\t\\end{lemma}\n   283\t\\begin{proof}\n   284\t\t\\uses{lem:distance-projective-left-square-root}\n   285\t\tStated in the source without proof; it is Fact 4.14 of \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. Write the off-diagonal mass $\\sum_{a \\ne b} \\bra{\\psi} A^x_a \\ot B^x_b \\ket{\\psi}$ as an inner product involving $(A^x_a \\ot \\Id - \\Id \\ot B^x_a)\\ket{\\psi}$, using projectivity of one of the two families, and apply the Cauchy--Schwarz inequality; this route costs a square root in the error. Only the branch in which $\\{A^x_a\\}$ is the projective family is available, as Lemma~\\ref{lem:agreement-projective-left}; the branch in which only $\\{B^x_a\\}$ is projective is still open.\n   286\t\\end{proof}\n   287\t\n   288\t\\begin{lemma}[Left-projective branch of Lemma~\\ref{fact:agreement-one-sided}]\\label{lem:agreement-projective-left}\n   289\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.consistencyDefect_le_sqrt_of_projective_left}\n   290\t\t\\leanok\n   291\t\t\\uses{def:consistency, def:povm-distance}\n   292\t\tThis entry states only the branch of Lemma~\\ref{fact:agreement-one-sided} in which the left family is the projective one; it is not the full disjunctive statement of the source. Let $\\mu$ be a probability distribution on $\\mathcal X$, let $\\ket{\\psi}$ be a unit vector, and let $\\{A^x_a\\}$ be a projective measurement for every $x$. Then\n   293\t\t\\[\n   294\t\t\t\\E_x \\sum_{a \\ne b} \\bra{\\psi} A^x_a \\ot B^x_b \\ket{\\psi}\n   295\t\t\t\\leq \\sqrt{2\\, \\E_x \\sum_a \\bigl\\lVert (A^x_a \\ot \\Id - \\Id \\ot B^x_a)\\ket{\\psi} \\bigr\\rVert^2}\\,;\n   296\t\t\\]\n   297\t\tin particular, if $A^x_a \\ot \\Id_B \\approx_\\delta \\Id_A \\ot B^x_a$, then $A^x_a \\ot \\Id_B \\simeq_{(2\\delta)^{1/2}} \\Id_A \\ot B^x_a$.\n   298\t\\end{lemma}\n   299\t\\begin{proof}\n   300\t\t\\leanok\n   301\t\t\\uses{lem:distance-projective-left-square-root, lem:distance-averaged-square-root}\n   302\t\tApply the pointwise estimate of Lemma~\\ref{lem:distance-projective-left-square-root} at each question $x$, then average over $x$ with Lemma~\\ref{lem:distance-averaged-square-root}.\n   303\t\\end{proof}\n   304\t\n   305\t\\begin{lemma}[Fact 4.20 in \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}]\\label{fact:add-a-proj}\n   306\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.opFamilyDistSq_mul_left_le}\n   307\t\t\\leanok\n   308\t\t\\uses{def:povm-distance}\n   309\t\tLet $\\mathcal A, \\mathcal B, \\mathcal C$ be finite sets, and let $\\mu$ be a distribution over question pairs $(x,y)$. Let $\\{A^x_{a,\\,b}\\}$ and $\\{B^x_{a,\\,b}\\}$ be operators whose outcomes range over the product set $\\mathcal A \\times \\mathcal B$. Suppose a set of operators $\\{C^y_{a,\\,c}\\}$, whose outcomes range over the product set $\\mathcal A \\times \\mathcal C$, satisfies the condition $\\sum_c (C^y_{a,\\,c})^\\dagger C^y_{a,\\,c} \\le \\Id$ for all $a$ and all $y$. If $A^x_{a,\\,b} \\approx_\\delta B^x_{a,\\,b}$ on average over $x$ sampled from the corresponding marginal of the distribution $\\mu$, then $C^y_{a,\\,c} A^x_{a,\\,b} \\approx_\\delta C^y_{a,\\,c} B^x_{a,\\,b}$ on average over $(x,y)$ sampled from $\\mu$.\n   310\t\\end{lemma}\n   311\t\\begin{proof}\n   312\t\t\\leanok\n   313\t\t\\uses{lem:distance-sum-contraction}\n   314\t\tFix questions $x, y$ and answers $a \\in \\mathcal A$, $b \\in \\mathcal B$. Then\n   315\t\t\\begin{align*}\n   316\t\t\t\\sum_c \\bigl\\lVert (C^y_{a,\\,c} A^x_{a,\\,b} - C^y_{a,\\,c} B^x_{a,\\,b})\\, \\ket{\\psi} \\bigr\\rVert^2\n   317\t\t\t&= \\bra{\\psi} (A^x_{a,\\,b} - B^x_{a,\\,b})^\\dagger \\Bigl( \\sum_c (C^y_{a,\\,c})^\\dagger C^y_{a,\\,c} \\Bigr) (A^x_{a,\\,b} - B^x_{a,\\,b}) \\ket{\\psi}\\\\\n   318\t\t\t&\\le \\bigl\\lVert (A^x_{a,\\,b} - B^x_{a,\\,b})\\, \\ket{\\psi} \\bigr\\rVert^2\\,,\n   319\t\t\\end{align*}\n   320\t\twhere the inequality uses the assumption $\\sum_c (C^y_{a,\\,c})^\\dagger C^y_{a,\\,c} \\le \\Id$. Summing over $a, b$ and averaging over $(x,y) \\sim \\mu$ yields the conclusion.\n   321\t\\end{proof}\n   322\t\n   323\t\\begin{lemma}[Multiplying by function-indexed operators]\\label{fact:add-a-proj2}\n   324\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.opFamilyDistSq_mul_funIndexed_le}\n   325\t\t\\leanok\n   326\t\t\\uses{def:povm-distance}\n   327\t\tLet $\\mathcal X, \\mathcal A$ be finite sets, and let $\\mathcal G$ be a set of functions $g \\colon \\mathcal X \\to \\mathcal A$. Let $\\{A^x_a\\}$ and $\\{B^x_a\\}$ be POVMs indexed by $\\mathcal X$ with outcomes in $\\mathcal A$. Let $\\{S^x_g\\}_{x \\in \\mathcal X,\\, g \\in \\mathcal G}$ be a set of operators such that for all $x \\in \\mathcal X$, $\\sum_g (S^x_g)^\\dagger S^x_g \\le \\Id$. If $A^x_a \\approx_\\delta B^x_a$ on average over $x$, then $S^x_g A^x_{g(x)} \\approx_\\delta S^x_g B^x_{g(x)}$.\n   328\t\\end{lemma}\n   329\t\\begin{proof}\n   330\t\t\\leanok\n   331\t\t\\uses{lem:distance-function-indexed-contraction}\n   332\t\tExpand the left-hand side and group the sum over $g$ according to the value $a = g(x)$:\n   333\t\t\\[\n   334\t\t\t\\E_x \\sum_g \\bigl\\lVert S^x_g (A^x_{g(x)} - B^x_{g(x)})\\, \\ket{\\psi} \\bigr\\rVert^2\n   335\t\t\t= \\E_x \\sum_a \\bra{\\psi} (A^x_a - B^x_a)^\\dagger \\Bigl( \\sum_{g\\,:\\,g(x)=a} (S^x_g)^\\dagger S^x_g \\Bigr) (A^x_a - B^x_a) \\ket{\\psi}\\,.\n   336\t\t\\]\n   337\t\t% The source (references/qpbt-paper/06_nonlocal_games_and_mipstar.tex:347-361) ends with\n   338\t\t% \"at most delta by assumption\"; under def:povm-distance the hypothesis only bounds this\n   339\t\t% quantity by O(delta), which is what the conclusion requires.\n   340\t\tSince $\\sum_{g\\,:\\,g(x)=a} (S^x_g)^\\dagger S^x_g \\le \\sum_g (S^x_g)^\\dagger S^x_g \\le \\Id$, this is at most $\\E_x \\sum_a \\lVert (A^x_a - B^x_a)\\ket{\\psi} \\rVert^2$, which is $O(\\delta)$ by the hypothesis $A^x_a \\approx_\\delta B^x_a$ (Definition~\\ref{def:povm-distance}); this is exactly the bound required for the conclusion.\n   341\t\\end{proof}\n   342\t\n   343\t\\begin{lemma}[Absorbing an approximating family into a projective sum]\\label{lem:cool-closeness-fact}\n   344\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.opDistSq_sum_sub_mul_le_of_projective}\n   345\t\t\\leanok\n   346\t\t\\uses{def:povm-distance, def:povm-conventions}\n   347\t\tLet $\\mathcal X$ and $\\mathcal A$ be finite sets and $\\mu$ a distribution on $\\mathcal X$. Let $\\{A^x_a\\}_{a \\in \\mathcal A}$ be a projective measurement and let $\\{B^x_a\\}_{a \\in \\mathcal A}$ be a set of matrices. If $A^x_a \\approx_\\delta B^x_a$, then for all subsets $S \\subseteq \\mathcal A$ we have\n   348\t\t\\[\n   349\t\t\t\\sum_{a \\in S} A^x_a \\approx_\\delta \\sum_{a \\in S} A^x_a \\cdot B^x_a\\,.\n   350\t\t\\]\n   351\t\\end{lemma}\n   352\t\\begin{proof}\n   353\t\t\\leanok\n   354\t\t\\uses{lem:distance-projective-orthogonality,\n   355\t\t\tlem:distance-projective-sum}\n   356\t\tBy projectivity, $\\sum_{a \\in S} (A^x_a - A^x_a B^x_a) = \\sum_{a \\in S} A^x_a (A^x_a - B^x_a)$. Expanding the squared norm of the corresponding vector, the cross terms for distinct $a \\ne a' \\in S$ vanish because $A^x_a A^x_{a'} = 0$, so\n   357\t\t\\begin{align*}\n   358\t\t\t\\E_x \\Bigl\\lVert \\sum_{a \\in S} \\bigl(A^x_a - A^x_a B^x_a\\bigr) \\ket{\\psi} \\Bigr\\rVert^2\n   359\t\t\t&= \\E_x \\sum_{a \\in S} \\bra{\\psi} (A^x_a - B^x_a)^\\dagger A^x_a (A^x_a - B^x_a) \\ket{\\psi}\\\\\n   360\t\t\t&\\le \\E_x \\sum_a \\lVert (A^x_a - B^x_a)\\ket{\\psi} \\rVert^2\\,,\n   361\t\t\\end{align*}\n   362\t\t% Under def:povm-distance the closeness hypothesis bounds the final quantity by\n   363\t\t% O(delta), not by delta itself; the source\n   364\t\t% (references/qpbt-paper/06_nonlocal_games_and_mipstar.tex:364-380) elides this.\n   365\t\twhere the last step uses $A^x_a \\le \\Id$. All expectations are taken according to $\\mu$, and the last quantity is $O(\\delta)$ by the assumption $A^x_a \\approx_\\delta B^x_a$ (Definition~\\ref{def:povm-distance}), which is the bound required for the conclusion.\n   366\t\\end{proof}\n   367\t\n   368\t\\begin{lemma}[Triangle inequality; Fact 4.28 in \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}]\\label{fact:triangle}\n   369\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.opFamilyDistSq_le_of_le_of_le}\n   370\t\t\\leanok\n   371\t\t\\uses{def:povm-distance}\n   372\t\tIf $A^x_a \\approx_\\delta B^x_a$ and $B^x_a \\approx_{\\eps} C^x_a$, then $A^x_a \\approx_{\\delta+\\eps} C^x_a$.\n   373\t\\end{lemma}\n   374\t\\begin{proof}\n   375\t\t\\leanok\n   376\t\t\\uses{def:povm-distance}\n   377\t\tStated in the source without proof; it is Fact 4.28 of \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. For every $x$ and $a$, $\\lVert (A^x_a - C^x_a)\\ket{\\psi} \\rVert^2 \\le 2\\lVert (A^x_a - B^x_a)\\ket{\\psi} \\rVert^2 + 2\\lVert (B^x_a - C^x_a)\\ket{\\psi} \\rVert^2$. Averaging over $x$ and summing over $a$ gives the claim; the factor $2$ is absorbed by the $O(\\cdot)$ in Definition~\\ref{def:povm-distance}.\n   378\t\\end{proof}\n   379\t\n   380\t\\begin{lemma}[Triangle inequality for ``$\\simeq$''; Proposition 4.29 in \\cite{Ji2020LowIndividualDegree}]\\label{fact:triangle-for-simeq}\n   381\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.consistencyDefect_trans_le}\n   382\t\t\\leanok\n   383\t\t\\uses{def:consistency}\n   384\t\tIf $A^x_a \\ot \\Id_B \\simeq_{\\eps} \\Id_A \\ot B^x_a$, and $C^x_a \\ot \\Id_B \\simeq_\\delta \\Id_A \\ot B^x_a$, and $C^x_a \\ot \\Id_B \\simeq_\\gamma \\Id_A \\ot D^x_a$, then\n   385\t\t\\[\n   386\t\t\tA^x_a \\ot \\Id_B \\simeq_{\\eps + 2\\sqrt{\\delta+\\gamma}} \\Id_A \\ot D^x_a\\,.\n   387\t\t\\]\n   388\t\\end{lemma}\n   389\t\\begin{proof}\n   390\t\t\\leanok\n   391\t\t\\uses{fact:agreement, fact:triangle, lem:distance-overlap-gap,\n   392\t\t\tlem:distance-defect-overlap, lem:distance-symmetry}\n   393\t\tStated in the source without proof; it is Proposition 4.29 of \\cite{Ji2020LowIndividualDegree}. The two hypotheses involving $C$ convert, via Lemma~\\ref{fact:agreement}, into state-dependent closeness of $C^x_a \\ot \\Id_B$ to both $\\Id_A \\ot B^x_a$ and $\\Id_A \\ot D^x_a$, hence of $\\Id_A \\ot B^x_a$ to $\\Id_A \\ot D^x_a$ at cost $O(\\delta+\\gamma)$. The first consistency then transfers from $B$ to $D$ by expanding the off-rojectiveSetting.wrongFormMass_alice_le_error}\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch14_qpbt_observables.tex:574:\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.ProjectiveSetting.wrongForm