210:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_107","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba blueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex | sed -n '520,575p'\"","aggregated_output":"   520\t\tremaining outer pair collapses by idempotence to\n   521\t\t$\\sum_{g_k} G^{k,\\,x}_{g_k} = \\Id$.\n   522\t\\end{proof}\n   523\t\n   524\t% The source (references/qpbt-paper/06_nonlocal_games_and_mipstar.tex:504-505) quantifies\n   525\t% the pairwise-agreement hypothesis over every pair (x, y_1), including pairs carrying\n   526\t% probability zero under D, on which no conditional distribution of y_2 is defined; the\n   527\t% hypothesis is restated here over the pairs of positive marginal probability, the only\n   528\t% pairs the sampling in the hypothesis can refer to and the only ones the averaged\n   529\t% conclusion depends on. The NEEXP original (references/neexp-paper/\n   530\t% 05_quantum_preliminaries.tex:1000-1008, label fact:low-degree-sandwich-on-steroids)\n   531\t% conditions on a fixed question tuple with the same restriction left implicit.\n   532\t\\begin{lemma}[Pasting two consistent measurements; Fact 4.35 in \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}]\\label{lem:pasting}\n   533\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.pastedMeasurement}\n   534\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.exists_pasting_error}\n   535\t\t\\leanok\n   536\t\t\\uses{def:consistency, def:bracket, def:povm-conventions}\n   537\t\tLet $\\mathcal D$ be a distribution on $(x, y_1, y_2) \\in \\mathcal X \\times \\mathcal Y_1 \\times \\mathcal Y_2$. For $i \\in \\{1,2\\}$ let $\\mathcal G_i$ be a collection of functions $g_i \\colon \\mathcal Y_i \\to \\mathcal R_i$ and let $\\{(G_i)^x_g\\}_{g \\in \\mathcal G_i}$ be families of measurements such that $\\{(G_2)^x_g\\}_g$ is projective for every $x$. Suppose further that for every pair $(x, y_1)$ of positive probability under the marginal of $\\mathcal D$ on $\\mathcal X \\times \\mathcal Y_1$ (the only pairs for which the conditional distribution of $y_2$ under $\\mathcal D$ is defined; pairs of marginal probability zero are subject to no requirement) it holds that for $g_2 \\ne g_2' \\in \\mathcal G_2$ the probability, on average over $y_2$ chosen from $\\mathcal D$ conditioned on $(x, y_1)$, that $g_2(y_2) = g_2'(y_2)$ is at most $\\eta$. Let $\\{A^{x,y_1,y_2}_{a_1,\\,a_2}\\}$ be a family of projective measurements with outcomes $(a_1, a_2) \\in \\mathcal R_1 \\times \\mathcal R_2$ such that for $i \\in \\{1,2\\}$,\n   538\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:pasting-1}\n   539\t\t\tA^{x,y_1,y_2}_{a_i} \\ot \\Id \\simeq_\\delta \\Id \\ot (G_i)^x_{[\\operatorname{eval}_{y_i}(\\cdot)=a_i]}\n   540\t\t\\end{equation}\n   541\t\tand\n   542\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:pasting-2}\n   543\t\t\tA^{x,y_1,y_2}_{a_1,\\,a_2} \\ot \\Id \\simeq_\\delta \\Id \\ot A^{x,y_1,y_2}_{a_1,\\,a_2}\\,,\n   544\t\t\\end{equation}\n   545\t\twhere $A^{x,y_1,y_2}_{a_i}$ denotes the marginal on the $i$-th part of the outcome (Definition~\\ref{def:povm-conventions}). Define a family of measurements $\\{J^x_{g_1,\\,g_2}\\}$ by\n   546\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:pasting-2a}\n   547\t\t\tJ^x_{g_1,\\,g_2} = (G_2)^x_{g_2}\\, (G_1)^x_{g_1}\\, (G_2)^x_{g_2}\\,.\n   548\t\t\\end{equation}\n   549\t\tThen there is a\n   550\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:def-deltap}\n   551\t\t\t\\delta_{\\mathrm{pasting}} = \\delta_{\\mathrm{pasting}}(\\eta, \\delta) = \\poly(\\eta, \\delta)\n   552\t\t\\end{equation}\n   553\t\tsuch that\n   554\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:pasting-3}\n   555\t\t\tA^{x,y_1,y_2}_{a_1,\\,a_2} \\ot \\Id \\simeq_{\\delta_{\\mathrm{pasting}}} \\Id \\ot J^x_{[\\operatorname{eval}_{y_1}(\\cdot)=a_1,\\, \\operatorname{eval}_{y_2}(\\cdot)=a_2]}\\,.\n   556\t\t\\end{equation}\n   557\t\tNeither the exponent nor the constant in $\\poly(\\eta, \\delta)$ is made explicit in the source; both are universal constants inherited from the proof of Fact 4.35 in \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}.\n   558\t\\end{lemma}\n   559\t\\begin{proof}\n   560\t\tStated in the source without proof, as an import of Fact 4.35 of \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. The measurement $J^x$ sandwiches the family $(G_1)^x$ between two copies of the projective family $(G_2)^x$. The consistency hypotheses \\eqref{eq:pasting-1} and the self-consistency \\eqref{eq:pasting-2} allow the joint measurement $A^{x,y_1,y_2}$ to be compared with the sandwiched product one factor at a time, and the $\\eta$-bound on the pairwise agreement of distinct functions in $\\mathcal G_2$ controls the error terms arising when two different outcomes $g_2 \\ne g_2'$ agree at the sampled point $y_2$.\n   561\t\\end{proof}\n   562\t\n   563\t\\begin{lemma}[Formalization support for Lemma~\\ref{lem:pasting}]\n   564\t\t\\label{lem:pasting-measurement}\n   565\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.pastedMeasurement_isMeasurement}\n   566\t\t\\leanok\n   567\t\t\\uses{def:povm-conventions}\n   568\t\tThis formalization-only auxiliary is not a named statement of the paper; it\n   569\t\tisolates the assertion, left implicit in~\\eqref{eq:pasting-2a}, that the\n   570\t\tpasted family is a POVM. If $\\{(G_1)^x_{g_1}\\}_{g_1}$ is a measurement and\n   571\t\t$\\{(G_2)^x_{g_2}\\}_{g_2}$ is a projective measurement, then every operator\n   572\t\t$J^x_{g_1,\\,g_2}$ is positive and\n   573\t\t$\\sum_{g_1,\\,g_2} J^x_{g_1,\\,g_2} = \\Id$.\n   574\t\\end{lemma}\n   575\t\\begin{proof}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
