212:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_108","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba blueprint/src/chapter/ch14_qpbt_observables.tex | sed -n '370,475p'\"","aggregated_output":"   370\t\t$B^x = \\sum_a \\alpha_a B^x_a$. Then\n   371\t\t\\[\n   372\t\t\tA^x_a \\approx_\\delta B^x_a \\quad\\text{implies}\\quad A^x \\approx_{\\delta'} B^x\n   373\t\t\\]\n   374\t\tfor $\\delta' = |\\mathcal{A}| \\cdot \\delta$, where the hypothesis is the\n   375\t\tanswer-indexed closeness of Definition~\\ref{def:povm-distance} and the\n   376\t\tconclusion is the question-indexed closeness of\n   377\t\tDefinition~\\ref{def:approx-question-indexed-operators}.\n   378\t\\end{lemma}\n   379\t\\begin{proof}\n   380\t\t\\leanok\n   381\t\t\\uses{lem:povm-to-obs-operator-families}\n   382\t\tApply Lemma~\\ref{lem:povm-to-obs-operator-families} to the two families of\n   383\t\tPOVM effects.\n   384\t\\end{proof}\n   385\t\n   386\tThe following lemma shows that POVMs that are approximately projective, in\n   387\tthe sense of being self-consistent, are close to exact projective\n   388\tmeasurements.\n   389\t\n   390\t\\begin{lemma}[Orthonormalization lemma]\n   391\t\t\\label{lem:ortho}\n   392\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.exists_projective_close_of_consistent}\n   393\t\t\\leanok\n   394\t\t\\uses{def:consistency, def:povm-distance}\n   395\t\tLet $0 \\leq \\delta \\leq 1$. There exists a function\n   396\t\t$\\eta_{\\mathrm{ortho}}(\\delta) = O\\big(\\delta^{1/4}\\big)$ such that the\n   397\t\tfollowing holds. Let $\\ket{\\psi}$ be a state on\n   398\t\t$\\hilb_{\\mathsf{A}} \\ot \\hilb_{\\mathsf{B}}$ where $\\hilb_{\\mathsf{A}}$\n   399\t\tand $\\hilb_{\\mathsf{B}}$ are finite dimensional, and let $\\{Q_a\\}$ and\n   400\t\t$\\{R_a\\}$ be POVMs on $\\hilb_{\\mathsf{A}}$ and $\\hilb_{\\mathsf{B}}$\n   401\t\trespectively such that\n   402\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:cons-cond}\n   403\t\t\tQ_a \\ot \\Id_{\\mathsf{B}} \\,\\simeq_\\delta\\, \\Id_{\\mathsf{A}} \\ot R_a\\,.\n   404\t\t\\end{equation}\n   405\t\tThen there exists a projective measurement $\\{P_a\\}$ on\n   406\t\t$\\hilb_{\\mathsf{A}}$ such that, with respect to $\\ket{\\psi}$,\n   407\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:cons-ccl}\n   408\t\t\tP_a \\ot \\Id_{\\mathsf{B}} \\,\\approx_{\\eta_{\\mathrm{ortho}}(\\delta)}\\, Q_a \\ot \\Id_{\\mathsf{B}}\\,.\n   409\t\t\\end{equation}\n   410\t\\end{lemma}\n   411\t\\begin{proof}\n   412\t\t\\leanok\n   413\t\t\\uses{lem:ortho-explicit-constant}\n   414\t\tThe source imports this lemma: it first appears in~\\cite{KV11}, and a\n   415\t\tself-contained proof is given in the reference [ML20] cited\n   416\t\tin~\\cite{Ji2020MIPStar}. Here it is obtained from\n   417\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:ortho-explicit-constant}, which exhibits\n   418\t\t$\\eta_{\\mathrm{ortho}}(\\delta) = 220\\,\\delta^{1/4}$: passing to Alice's\n   419\t\treduced state, the rank-reduction and orthonormalization construction of\n   420\t\tChapter~\\ref{chap:projective} produces a projective family on\n   421\t\t$\\hilb_{\\mathsf{A}}$ close to $\\{Q_a\\}$, and completing that family at one\n   422\t\tfixed outcome yields the projective measurement $\\{P_a\\}$.\n   423\t\\end{proof}\n   424\t\n   425\t\\begin{lemma}[Formalization support for Lemma~\\ref{lem:ortho}]\n   426\t\t\\label{lem:ortho-explicit-constant}\n   427\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.projective_rounding_with_explicit_constant}\n   428\t\t\\leanok\n   429\t\t\\uses{def:consistency, def:povm-distance}\n   430\t\tThis formalization-only auxiliary is not a named statement of the paper; it is\n   431\t\tthe explicit-constant form of Lemma~\\ref{lem:ortho} from which that lemma is\n   432\t\tobtained. Let $\\ket{\\psi}$ be a unit vector on\n   433\t\t$\\hilb_{\\mathsf{A}} \\ot \\hilb_{\\mathsf{B}}$ with $\\hilb_{\\mathsf{A}}$ and\n   434\t\t$\\hilb_{\\mathsf{B}}$ finite dimensional, let $\\{Q_a\\}$ and $\\{R_a\\}$ be POVMs\n   435\t\ton $\\hilb_{\\mathsf{A}}$ and $\\hilb_{\\mathsf{B}}$ respectively, and let\n   436\t\t$\\delta \\geq 0$ satisfy\n   437\t\t\\[\n   438\t\t\tQ_a \\ot \\Id_{\\mathsf{B}} \\,\\simeq_\\delta\\, \\Id_{\\mathsf{A}} \\ot R_a\\,.\n   439\t\t\\]\n   440\t\tThen there is a projective measurement $\\{P_a\\}$ on $\\hilb_{\\mathsf{A}}$ with\n   441\t\t\\[\n   442\t\t\td_\\psi\\bigl(P_a \\ot \\Id_{\\mathsf{B}},\\, Q_a \\ot \\Id_{\\mathsf{B}}\\bigr)^2\n   443\t\t\t\\leq 220\\,\\delta^{1/4}\\,.\n   444\t\t\\]\n   445\t\\end{lemma}\n   446\t\\begin{proof}\n   447\t\t\\leanok\n   448\t\t\\uses{lem:projective-low-rank-sum, lem:Q-completeness, lem:P-Q-approx,\n   449\t\t\tdef:projective-P}\n   450\t\tWork with Alice's reduced state $\\varphi$, for which the consistency\n   451\t\thypothesis gives $\\sum_a \\bra{\\varphi} Q_a - Q_a^2 \\ket{\\varphi} \\leq 2\\delta$.\n   452\t\tWhen $\\delta^{1/4} \\leq 1/24$, rank reduction\n   453\t\t(Lemma~\\ref{lem:projective-low-rank-sum}) replaces $\\{Q_a\\}$ by a family of\n   454\t\tprojectors, Lemma~\\ref{lem:Q-completeness} shows that this family is almost\n   455\t\tcomplete on $\\varphi$, and the operator $P$ of\n   456\t\tDefinition~\\ref{def:projective-P} is projective and close to it by\n   457\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:P-Q-approx}. Completing $P$ at one fixed outcome and summing the\n   458\t\ttwo estimates gives the bound $220\\,\\delta^{1/4}$, the completion contributing\n   459\t\tthe residual mass controlled by Lemma~\\ref{lem:Q-completeness}. When\n   460\t\t$\\delta^{1/4} > 1/24$, the bound $220\\,\\delta^{1/4} > 9$ exceeds the universal\n   461\t\tbound $4$ on the distance between any two sub-measurements on a normalized\n   462\t\tstate, so the completion of the zero sub-measurement already satisfies the\n   463\t\tconclusion.\n   464\t\\end{proof}\n   465\t\n   466\t\\section{Strategies}\n   467\t\\label{sec:commutation}\n   468\t\n   469\t% In the source this setup --- fixing the parameter tuple, the strategy and\n   470\t% its success probability, and making the measurements projective via\n   471\t% Naimark's theorem --- is untitled prose at the head of the subsection\n   472\t% (references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex, lines\n   473\t% 155-171), not a numbered environment. It is recorded here as a standing\n   474\t% convention paragraph together with a lemma for the one mathematical step\n   475\t% it contains, the reduction to a projective strategy.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
214:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_109","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba references/qpbt-paper/06_nonlocal_games_and_mipstar.tex | sed -n '85,140p'; nl -ba references/qpbt-paper/06_nonlocal_games_and_mipstar.tex | sed -n '520,550p'; nl -ba references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex | sed -n '125,160p'\"","aggregated_output":"    85\t  Hilbert space $\\mH$, that is invariant under permutation of the two factors,\n    86\t  and the measurement operators of both players are identical.\n    87\t\\end{definition}\n    88\t\n    89\tWe often specify symmetric games $\\game$ and symmetric strategies $\\strategy$ using\n    90\t  a compact notation: we write $\\game = (\\cal{X}, \\cal{A}, \\mu, D)$ and\n    91\t  $\\strategy = (\\ket{\\psi}, M)$ where $M$ denotes the set of measurement\n    92\t  operators for both players.\n    93\t\n    94\t\\begin{lemma}\\label{lem:symmetric-strat}\n    95\t  Let $\\game = (\\cal{X}, \\cal{A},  \\mu, D)$ be a symmetric game\n    96\t  such that $\\val^*(\\game) = 1-\\eps$ for some $\\eps\\geq 0$.\n    97\t  Then there exists a symmetric and projective strategy\n    98\t  $\\strategy=(\\ket{\\psi},M)$ such that $\\val^*(\\game,\\strategy)\\geq 1-\\eps$.\n    99\t\\end{lemma}\n   100\t\n   101\t\\begin{proof}\n   102\t  By definition for any $\\eps'>\\eps$ there exists a strategy $\\strategy'= (\\ket{\\psi'},A,B)$ such\n   103\t  that $\\val^*(\\game,\\strategy')\\geq 1-\\eps'$.\n   104\tUsing Naimark's theorem (Theorem~\\ref{thm:naimark}) we can assume\n   105\twithout loss of generality that $\\ket{\\psi'}\\in\\C_{\\alice'}^d\\otimes \\C_{\\bob'}^d$ for some integer $d$\n   106\t  and that for every $x$, $A^x$ and $B^x$ are projective measurements.\n   107\tLet\n   108\t  \\[\n   109\t    \\ket{\\psi} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\big( \\ket{0}_\\alice \\ket{1}_\\bob\n   110\t    \\ket{\\psi'}_{\\alice'\\bob'} + \\ket{1}_\\alice \\ket{0}_\\bob\n   111\t    \\ket{\\psi'_\\tau}_{\\alice'\\bob'}\\big) \\in (\\C_{\\alice}^2 \\otimes\n   112\t    \\C_{\\alice'}^d) \\otimes (\\C_\\bob^2 \\otimes \\C_{\\bob'}^d)\\;,\n   113\t  \\]\n   114\t  where $\\ket{\\psi'_\\tau}$ is obtained from $\\ket{\\psi}$ by permuting the two\n   115\t  players' registers.\n   116\t  Observe that $\\ket{\\psi}$ is invariant under permutation of $\\alice\\alice'$\n   117\t  and $\\bob\\bob'$.\n   118\t\n   119\t  For any question $x\\in \\cal{X}$ define the measurement $M^x = \\{M^x_a\\}_{a \\in \\cal{A}}$\n   120\t  acting on the Hilbert space $\\C^2 \\otimes \\C^d$ as follows:\n   121\t  \\[\n   122\t  \tM^x_a = \\ketbra{0}{0} \\otimes A^x_a + \\ketbra{1}{1} \\otimes B^x_a \\;,\n   123\t  \\]\n   124\t\ti.e.\\ for $\\ket{\\varphi} \\in \\C^d$, $M^x_a(\\ket{0}\\ket{\\varphi})=\\ket{0}A^x_a\\ket{\\varphi}$ and $M^x_a(\\ket{1}\\ket{\\varphi})=\\ket{1}B^x_a\\ket{\\varphi}$.\n   125\t  When Alice receives question $x$, she measures $M^x$ on registers $\\alice \\alice'$, and when Bob receives \n   126\t  question $y$, he measures $M^y$ on registers $\\bob \\bob'$.\n   127\tUsing that by assumption the decision predicate $D$ for $\\game$ is symmetric,\n   128\t  it is not hard to verify that $\\val^*(\\game,\\strategy) =\n   129\t  \\val^*(\\game,\\strategy')$.\n   130\t\\end{proof}\n   131\t\n   132\t\\begin{definition}\n   133\t  \\label{def:comm-strategy}\n   134\t  Let $\\game = (\\cal{X}, \\cal{Y}, \\cal{A}, \\cal{B}, \\mu, D)$ be a game, and let\n   135\t  $\\strategy = (\\ket{\\psi}, A, B)$ be a strategy for $\\game$ such that  $\\mH_A = \\mH_B$.\n   136\t  Let $S \\subseteq \\cal{X} \\times \\cal{Y}$ denote the support of the question\n   137\t  distribution $\\mu$, i.e.\\ the set of $(x,y)$ such that $\\mu(x,y)>0$.\n   138\t  We say that $\\strategy$ is a \\emph{commuting strategy for $\\game$} if for all\n   139\t  question pairs $(x, y) \\in S$, we have $[A^x_a, B^y_b] = 0$ \n   140\t  for all $a\n   520\t\\delta_{pasting} \\,=\\, \\delta_{pasting}(\\eta,\\delta)\\,=\\, \\poly(\\eta,\\delta)\n   521\t\\end{equation} such that \n   522\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:pasting-3}\n   523\t A^{x,y_1,y_2}_{a_1,a_2} \\otimes \\Id \\simeq_{\\delta_{pasting}} \\Id \\otimes J^x_{[\\eval_{y_1}(\\cdot)=a_1,\\eval_{y_2}(\\cdot) = a_2]}\\;.\n   524\t\\end{equation}\n   525\t\\end{lemma}\n   526\t\n   527\t\n   528\t\n   529\t\n   530\t\n   531\t\\begin{lemma}[Fact~4.32 of \\cite{NW19}]\n   532\t  \\label{lem:close-strategies-have-close-values}\n   533\t  Let $\\game$ be a nonlocal game, and let $\\strategy, \\strategy'$ be\n   534\t  two strategies that are $\\delta$-close (in the sense of\n   535\t  \\Cref{def:strategy-distance}) for $\\delta \\in [0,1]$, and use the\n   536\t  same state $\\ket{\\psi}$. If either $\\strategy$ or $\\strategy'$ is\n   537\t  projective, then $|\\val^*(\\game,\\strategy) - \\val^*(\\game,\n   538\t  \\strategy')| \\leq O(\\delta^{1/2})$.\n   539\t\\end{lemma}\n   540\t\n   541\t\\subsection{The class $\\MIP^*$}\n   542\t\n   543\tThe complexity class $\\MIP^*$ of \\emph{multi-prover interactive proof\n   544\t  systems with entangled provers} is the class of languages that can be\n   545\tdecided by a \\emph{proof system} in which a polynomial-time classical\n   546\t\\emph{verifier} interacts with noncommunicating, computationally unbounded\n   547\t \\emph{provers} who may share a \\emph{finite-dimensional} entangled state. In general there may be polynomially many provers,\n   548\tand the verifier may interact with them over polynomially many\n   549\trounds. In each round, the verifier generates a question for\n   550\teach prover and receives a response from them. At the end of\n   125\t\t\t\\E_x \\norm{(A^x - B^x) \\ket{\\psi}}^2 &= \\E_x \\Big\\|\\sum_a \\alpha_a (A^x_a - B^x_a) \\ket{\\psi}\\Big\\|^2 \\\\\n   126\t\t\t&\\leq \\E_x \\Big( \\sum_a \\norm{ (A^x_a - B^x_a) \\ket{\\psi}} \\Big)^2 \\\\\n   127\t\t\t&\\leq \\E_x |\\mathcal{A}| \\cdot \\sum_a \\norm{ (A^x_a - B^x_a) \\ket{\\psi}}^2 \\\\\n   128\t\t\t&\\leq |\\mathcal{A}| \\cdot \\delta\\;.\n   129\t\t\\end{align*}\n   130\tThe first inequality follows from the triangle inequality, the second inequality follows from Cauchy-Schwarz, and the last inequality follows from the assumption that $A^x_a \\approx_\\delta B^x_a$. \n   131\t\\end{proof}\n   132\t\n   133\t\n   134\tThe following lemma shows that POVMs that are approximately projective (in the sense of being self-consistent) are close to exact projective measurements. The lemma first appears in~\\cite{KV11}; for a self-contained proof, see~\\cite{ML20}.\n   135\t\n   136\t\\begin{lemma}[Orthonormalization lemma]\\label{lem:ortho}\n   137\tLet $0\\leq \\delta\\leq 1$. There exists a function $\\eta_{ortho}(\\delta)\\,=\\, O\\big(\\delta^{1/4}\\big)$ such that the following holds.  Let $\\ket{\\psi}$ be a state on $\\mH_\\alice \\otimes \\mH_\\bob$ where $\\mH_\\alice,\\mH_\\bob$ are finite dimensional. Let $\\{Q_a\\}$ and $\\{R_a\\}$ be POVM on $\\mH_\\alice$ and $\\mH_\\bob$ respectively such that \n   138\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:cons-cond} \n   139\tQ_a \\abc R_a \\,.\n   140\t\\end{equation}\n   141\tThen there exists a projective measurement $\\{P_a\\}$ such that\n   142\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:cons-ccl}\n   143\t  P_a \\otimes \\Id_\\bob \\,\\approx_{\\eta_{ortho}} Q_a \\otimes \\Id_\\bob \\;.\n   144\t\\end{equation}\n   145\t\\end{lemma}\n   146\t\n   147\t\n   148\t\n   149\t\n   150\t\n   151\t\n   152\t\n   153\t\n   154\t\n   155\t \\newcommand{\\acom}{\\typestyle{Anticom}}\n   156\t\n   157\t\\subsection{Strategies}\n   158\t\\label{sec:commutation}\n   159\t\n   160\t  Let $\\qldparams = (q,m,d)$ be an admissible parameter tuple (Definition~\\ref{def:admissible}) and let\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
